{"updated":"2026-02-09T12:09:38.922906+00:00","created":"2021-12-22T03:01:37.985851+00:00","uuid":"a6bdff7c-ee50-4898-a2e6-98485b86a9ec","id":"1994913","revision_id":8,"metadata":{"publication_info":[{"year":2023,"artid":"4","page_start":"4","journal_issue":"1","journal_title":"Ann.PDE","journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1834781"},"journal_volume":"9"}],"documents":[{"key":"a320ef5169e8f518cd4959cf47af091c","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/a320ef5169e8f518cd4959cf47af091c","filename":"document","fulltext":true}],"citation_count_without_self_citations":13,"citation_count":13,"dois":[{"value":"10.1007/s40818-022-00144-3","source":"Springer"}],"titles":[{"title":"Naked Singularities in the Einstein-Euler System","source":"Springer"},{"title":"Naked singularities in the Einstein-Euler system","source":"arXiv"}],"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","authors":[{"uuid":"29fea557-3f3f-4f90-8a59-4c750b2a7e52","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/2154501"},"full_name":"Guo, Yan","signature_block":"Gy","raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Division of Applied Mathematics, Brown University, 02912 Providence, RI, USA"}],"affiliations_identifiers":[{"value":"grid.40263.33","schema":"GRID"}]},{"uuid":"5c2ddc79-5032-469b-9408-c2ee985a7053","emails":["m.hadzic@ucl.ac.uk"],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1873117"},"full_name":"Hadzic, Mahir","signature_block":"HADSACm","raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Department of Mathematics, University College London, London, UK"}],"affiliations_identifiers":[{"value":"grid.83440.3b","schema":"GRID"}]},{"uuid":"b99c4ce0-bb1e-43ad-8fdc-8e6a2a843eae","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/2675922"},"full_name":"Jang, Juhi","signature_block":"JANGj","raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Department of Mathematics, University of Southern California, 90089 Los Angeles, CA, USA"},{"value":"Korea Institute for Advanced Study, Seoul, Korea"}],"affiliations_identifiers":[{"value":"grid.42505.36","schema":"GRID"},{"value":"grid.249961.1","schema":"GRID"}]}],"curated":false,"license":[{"url":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","license":"CC-BY-4.0","imposing":"Springer"},{"url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","license":"arXiv nonexclusive-distrib 1.0","material":"preprint"}],"texkeys":["Guo:2021cst"],"citeable":true,"imprints":[{"date":"2023-02-07"}],"keywords":[{"value":"Self-similar solutions","source":"author"},{"value":"Einstein-Euler system","source":"author"},{"value":"Implosion","source":"author"},{"value":"Naked singularities","source":"author"}],"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"In 1990, based on numerical and formal asymptotic analysis, Ori and Piran predicted the existence of selfsimilar spacetimes, called relativistic Larson-Penston solutions, that can be suitably flattened to obtain examples of spacetimes that dynamically form naked singularities from smooth initial data, and solve the radially symmetric Einstein-Euler system. Despite its importance, a rigorous proof of the existence of such spacetimes has remained elusive, in part due to the complications associated with the analysis across the so-called sonic hypersurface. We provide a rigorous mathematical proof. Our strategy is based on a delicate study of nonlinear invariances associated with the underlying non-autonomous dynamical system to which the problem reduces after a selfsimilar reduction. Key technical ingredients are a monotonicity lemma tailored to the problem, an ad hoc shooting method developed to construct a solution connecting the sonic hypersurface to the so-called Friedmann solution, and a nonlinear argument to construct the maximal analytic extension of the solution. Finally, we reformulate the problem in double-null gauge to flatten the selfsimilar profile and thus obtain an asymptotically flat spacetime with an isolated naked singularity.","source":"Springer"},{"value":"In 1990, based on numerical and formal asymptotic analysis, Ori and Piran predicted the existence of self-similar spacetimes, called relativistic Larson-Penston solutions, that can be suitably flattened to obtain examples of spacetimes that dynamically form naked singularities from smooth initial data, and solve the radially symmetric Einstein-Euler system. Despite its importance, a rigorous proof of the existence of such spacetimes has remained elusive, in part due to the complications associated with the analysis across the so-called sonic hypersurface. We provide a rigorous mathematical proof. Our strategy is based on a delicate study of nonlinear invariances associated with the underlying non-autonomous dynamical system to which the problem reduces after a self-similar reduction. Key technical ingredients are a monotonicity lemma tailored to the problem, an ad hoc shooting method developed to construct a solution connecting the sonic hypersurface to the so-called Friedmann solution, and a nonlinear argument to construct the maximal analytic extension of the solution. Finally, we reformulate the problem in double-null gauge to truncate the self-similar profile and thus obtain an asymptotically flat spacetime with an isolated naked singularity.","source":"arXiv"}],"references":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1307207"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[1] H. Bondi, \"Spherically symmetrical models in general relativity\", Mon.Not.Roy.Astron.Soc.,107,410-425, DOI: 10.1093/mnras/107.5-6.410","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1093/mnras/107.5-6.410"],"label":"1","title":{"title":"Spherically symmetrical models in general relativity"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Bondi, H."}],"publication_info":{"year":1947,"page_end":"425","page_start":"410","journal_title":"Mon.Not.Roy.Astron.Soc.","journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1214577"},"journal_volume":"107"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/471580"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[2] BJ Carr, AA Coley, \"Self-similarity in general relativity\", Class.Quant.Grav.,16,R31, DOI: 10.1088/0264-9381/16/7/201","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1088/0264-9381/16/7/201"],"label":"2","title":{"title":"Self-similarity in general relativity"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Carr, BJ"},{"full_name":"Coley, AA"}],"publication_info":{"year":1999,"artid":"R31","page_start":"R31","journal_title":"Class.Quant.Grav.","journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1214779"},"journal_volume":"16"}}},{"raw_refs":[{"value":"[3] BJ Carr, AA Coley, M. Goliath, US Nilsson, \"Critical phenomena and a new class of self-similar spherically symmetric perfect-fluid solutions\", Phys.Rev.D,61,1-5","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"label":"3","title":{"title":"Critical phenomena and a new class of self-similar spherically symmetric perfect-fluid solutions"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Carr, BJ"},{"full_name":"Coley, AA"},{"full_name":"Goliath, M."},{"full_name":"Nilsson, US"},{"full_name":"Uggla, C."}],"publication_info":{"year":2000,"page_end":"5","page_start":"1","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.D","journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1613970"},"journal_volume":"61"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/596049"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[4] BJ Carr, C. Gundlach: Spacetime structure of self-similar spherically symmetric perfect fluid solutions Phys, 2003, Rev. D 2003 67 1971907 , DOI: 10.1103/PhysRevD.67.024035","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1103/PhysRevD.67.024035"]}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/211917"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[5] D. Christodoulou, \"Violation of cosmic censorship in the gravitational collapse of a dust cloud\", Commun.Math.Phys.,93,171-195, DOI: 10.1007/BF01223743","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1007/BF01223743"],"label":"5","title":{"title":"Violation of cosmic censorship in the gravitational collapse of a dust cloud"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Christodoulou, D."}],"publication_i